{"cross_mech":[],"family":{"alphafold_models":3297,"candidate_reasons":["pfam_previous_unknown_name"],"characterization_status":"","context_evidence_count":null,"cross_mech":{"example_proteins":0,"protein_traits_record":false,"records_by_mech":{},"scanned":false},"curation_status":"SEEDED","demotion_reasons":[],"description":"This entry represents the seven transmembrane domain of wntless (WLS) from Drosophila and its homologues, also known as MIG-14 in C.elegans which regulates endocytosis and secretion of the wnt ligand egl-20[[cite:PUB00058252]]. Newly synthesised EGL-20/Wnt binds to MIG-14 in the Golgi, targetting the Wnt to the cell membrane for secretion. AP-2-mediated endocytosis and retromer retrieval at the sorting endosome would recycle MIG-14 to the Golgi, where it can bind to EGL-20/Wnt for next cycle of secretion [[cite:PUB00058252],[cite:PUB00154330]]. WLS is a membrane trafficking and secretion chaperone for lipidated Wnt signaling proteins [[cite:PUB00151072]]. It regulates Wnt proteins sorting and secretion in a feedback regulatory mechanism [[cite:PUB00151072],[cite:PUB00061859]]. This entry also includes TMEM181, a cell surface protein associated with Escherichia coli CDT toxicity [[cite:PUB00151072]]. Members of this entry are homologous to GPR180 and TMEM145, and together with TMEM87A/B, GPR107 and GPR108, they have been described as GOLD domain seven-transmembrane helix (GOST) proteins given their domain architecture similarities. They all consist of a GOLD domain atop of a seven-transmembrane helix domain [[cite:PUB00151072]].","domain_architectures":54,"interpro_id":"IPR047843","known_evidence_count":null,"matches":3913,"name":"Wntless-like, transmembrane domain","partial_evidence_count":null,"pfam_id":"PF06664","proteins":3813,"proteomes":1345,"short_name":"WLS-like_TM","source_url":"https://www.ebi.ac.uk/interpro/api/entry/pfam/PF06664","structures":9,"taxa":4379,"unknown_status":"EX_DUF"},"inputs":{"cross_mech":"cross-mech-duf-examples-202